WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.117 --> 00:00:02.100
- [Shelby] For the first
time in four decades,

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.820
the US Army unveiled a wholly
new armored fighting vehicle,

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.350
the M10 Booker.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.020
- [Scott] It has a fully
traversable turret.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.440
It is armed with a 105
millimeter main gun.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.420
- [Shelby] Some army
officials have called it.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.680
- A new light tank.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.060
- [Shelby] But many say the mission

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.350
and protection of the Booker

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.120
is very different from a tank.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.670
Here's how it compares
to tanks like the Abrams,

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.800
which the US is sending to Ukraine

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.890
and why the army has
invested more than $1 billion

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.053
to equip itself with this new vehicle.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.270
The Booker looks very
similar to the Abrams.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.660
Both are fully tracked vehicles
with a four-person crew,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.530
360-degree turret, large caliber main gun

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.780
and a commander's
independent thermal viewer

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.290
that allows visibility at night
and in multiple directions.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.540
But the Booker is just
over half the weight

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.340
of the latest Abrams tank.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.930
- The battlefield role

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:59.160
for the M10 will be to support
deployed infantry forces.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.950
The Booker basically
gives you armor support

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.690
that you would not have otherwise had.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:07.170
It can carry a wider variety of munitions.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.470
It can go places that an
up armored Humvee can't go.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:11.303
- [Shelby] Tracked vehicles

00:01:11.303 --> 00:01:12.960
distribute weight more effectively

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.760
for better off-road performance

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.710
and the Booker's road
wheels are even lighter

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.530
than previous tracked
vehicles of a similar size.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:23.280
This allows it to move easily
to and around the battlefield,

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.803
a common choke point for
heavier tanks like the Abrams.

00:01:25.803 --> 00:01:26.636
(chain clanging)

00:01:26.636 --> 00:01:28.710
- There will be bridges, for example,

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.880
that could support a Booker
but not support an Abrams.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.710
- [Shelby] It also means
the Booker can be deployed

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.050
by aircraft relatively easily.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:37.883
- [Soldier] Rotate.

00:01:37.883 --> 00:01:41.040
- [Shelby] For the 31 M1A1
Abrams promised to Ukraine,

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.920
delivery could be one of
the biggest challenges.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.300
Only one Abrams can fit

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.993
inside this C-17 Globemaster
compared to two Bookers.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.470
But in order to get a
vehicle in this weight class,

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.650
the army had to be more strategic
about the level of armor.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:56.640
Just look at the turret

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.860
on the Booker compared to the Abrams.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.150
- [Scott] You can see from the roof

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:03.060
on the turret in particular
is actually armor.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.620
- [Shelby] The turret connects the gun

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.330
to the main body of the vehicle.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.150
- The turret front is usually
thought to be the part

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:13.110
of the vehicle that is most
likely to be hit by an enemy.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.690
Abrams' turret is very well protected.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.450
Booker's turret is probably
fairly well protected

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.790
but the expectation is not there

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.640
that it would be as survivable as Abrams.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.020
- [Shelby] Even with less protection,

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.850
the Booker does bring new capabilities

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.040
to infantry forces with its main gun,

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.993
a nearly 105-millimeter cannon.

00:02:31.993 --> 00:02:32.850
(cannon booming)

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:36.300
That's smaller than the Abrams
120-millimeter main gun,

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.260
but more powerful

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.500
than the 25 millimeter
cannon on the M2 Bradley.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.650
- It gives you the flexibility
of firing projectiles

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:46.500
that are specifically
designed to penetrate armor.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.180
- [Shelby] And the Booker
also comes equipped

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.430
with a highly accurate targeting system.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.320
- Essentially, on each of these vehicles,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.760
you have a computer.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.850
That computer can be
used to detect the range

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.800
to the target using a laser

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.310
and it can calculate where to
fire the gun to hit a target,

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:05.340
including if it's moving and
including if you're moving.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.050
- [Shelby] But less
armor might also explain

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:09.720
a key difference between
the two: engine placement.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.550
- In the case of the
Booker, that would mean

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.070
that the engine sits in front of the crew

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.680
and potentially provides
additional protection to them.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.080
So if the vehicle is hit from the front,

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.250
then there is a chance

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:23.370
that the crew would be more
likely to survive a hit.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.580
Abrams simply just has a
great deal of front armor.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:30.270
It balances it by having
the engine at the back.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.730
- [Shelby] Compared to the
Abrams' gas turbine engine.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:35.460
- Burning like 12 gallons of fuel an hour

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:36.930
just to be turned on.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.300
- [Shelby] The Booker's 800
horsepower diesel engine

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:43.500
is meant to extend the fuel
time for a full 24 hours.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:47.280
- When the vehicle is idling,
it will burn much less fuel.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.310
- All of these factors,
weight, armor, main gun

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.520
and engine play into how the
two differ in deployment.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.560
The Booker is not necessarily designed

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.170
to go toe to toe with
an enemy's heavy armor.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:02.820
- Top tier main battle tanks,
adversary armored vehicles

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.130
are not what you would want a Booker for.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.410
This is why we have Abrams

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:09.930
and the armored brigade combat team.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.460
- [Shelby] The armored brigade combat team

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.140
is considered a heavy force.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.120
The Booker, on the other
hand, is meant to work

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.420
with the ground troops or the
infantry brigade combat team.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.640
Military experts say this team is designed

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:21.780
for quick deployment

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.300
and is able to operate in
highly restrictive terrain,

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.480
like jungles, forests,
mountains, and cities.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:28.890
- The advantage of infantry

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:30.870
is that they can go lots of places.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.270
They have lots of ability to see things.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.640
They can sneak around more effectively.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.100
If you are fighting in an environment

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.750
that has very difficult terrain,

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.340
they can then communicate

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:45.450
where the most dangerous
targets are to your tank.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.490
- [Shelby] And the crew
members operating this vehicle

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.010
will find it rather familiar
because both the Booker

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.470
and the Abrams are made
by the same company,

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:54.450
General Dynamics Land Systems,

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.480
so they do share some
features in helpful ways.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.420
- [Scott] We're told that
they have similar controls

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:01.740
for the crew members.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.330
- [Shelby] These shared
characteristics will allow

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:04.860
for more seamless training.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.090
- [Scott] It will be very familiar

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:09.810
to tankers who will basically
use similar crew drill.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:10.650
- [Shelby] And both the Booker

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:13.320
and the Abrams have
compartmentalized ammunition,

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.510
which is a design intended
to protect the crew

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.233
if the ammunition explodes.

00:05:17.233 --> 00:05:18.066
(ammunition clicking)

00:05:18.066 --> 00:05:19.830
This is what helps these vehicles avoid

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.980
the jack-in-the-box explosion
seen with many Russian tanks

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:24.570
during the war in Ukraine

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:27.390
where ammunition stored
around the turret exploded,

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:31.110
likely killing the crew and
causing the turret to fly off.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.300
But the arrival of the
Booker comes at a time

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:34.830
when new challenges have arisen

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.680
for tanks and combat vehicles alike.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.120
- [Scott] Modern armored vehicles

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.360
can be attacked from
literally any direction.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.890
Right now in Ukraine, vehicles
are worrying about artillery

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:50.340
and drones that might
attack the top of a vehicle.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:52.020
They have to deal with mines

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.970
that would attack the bottom of a vehicle,

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:56.010
and of course, direct
fire threats can come

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:57.690
from any direction.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.670
- [Shelby] The army grew
interested in building a vehicle

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:02.610
like the Booker just years
after Russia's annexation

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:04.830
of Crimea with experts noting the need

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:06.840
for more mobile-protected firepower

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.090
in light of modern Russian warfare

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:12.450
but actually making the Booker
a reality hasn't been cheap.

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:14.640
Last summer, General
Dynamics was contracted

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.640
for the production and
fielding of nearly 100 vehicles

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:21.840
in a $1.14 billion deal
before extending the contract

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:24.120
to include an additional 26 vehicles

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:26.673
and adding another $258 million.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:29.700
But the vehicle has run into issues.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:31.350
Early tests found high levels

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:33.840
of toxic fumes when firing the main gun.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.450
- The army has pretty high expectations

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:40.080
of how ready a vehicle should
be when it reaches soldiers.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:42.900
If there's any issue with soldier safety,

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.630
problem with the fumes from the main gun,

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:47.730
which they actually have
dealt with according

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:50.340
to the office that works
the acquisition on this,

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:51.780
they'll have to deal with that

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:54.240
before they can green light production.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:56.820
- [Shelby] The first unit
of M10 Bookers is expected

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.830
toward the end of 2025.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:03.000
Each of those systems is expected
to cost around $13 million

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:06.690
with plans for the cost to go
down as production increases.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:08.430
This price doesn't perfectly compare

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.710
to the estimated $10 million Abrams

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:13.710
because unlike the Abrams,
the Booker is a new build.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:16.710
- The difference between
the newest version of Abrams

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:20.778
and the original version
of Abrams is night and day.

00:07:20.778 --> 00:07:22.410
(cannon booming)

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:24.450
Abrams literally got a gun upgrade,

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:27.390
a different main gun
installed when it went

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.880
from the M1 to the M1A1.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:31.110
- [Shelby] But this first iteration

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:32.880
of the Booker fills an important gap

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.260
in the army's capabilities

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.410
and is intended to serve
as a long-term solution.

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:38.820
- When this thing is fielded,

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:42.540
there will almost certainly
be then subsequent changes.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:44.610
You give a vehicle like
this to a bunch of soldiers

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:46.950
and they will find ways to
break it that you need to fix

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:49.320
and they will find things
to improve about it

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.360
that will then go back up through the army

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:54.600
and ultimately hopefully
turn into a Booker A2

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:56.809
that is a better version.

00:07:56.809 --> 00:07:59.476
(pensive music)

